---
import Base from '../layouts/Base.astro';
import { href } from '../i18n/routes';

const lang = 'en' as const;
const page = 'platforms' as const;
---
<Base lang={lang} page={page}
  title="Supported platforms"
  description="GitHub and Azure DevOps, hosted or self-hosted, in all major browsers. See what's supported today and what's coming next.">
<section class="hband">
      <div class="in">
        <div class="stack g18 measure">
          <span class="eyebrow acc">compatibility:</span>
          <h1 style="font-size:clamp(29px,3.8vw,41px);font-weight:700;letter-spacing:-.031em;line-height:1.05">Supported platforms</h1>
          <p class="lede">The extension works in all major browsers across both hosted and self-hosted code review platforms.</p>
        </div>

        <div class="avail">
          <div class="av-card">
            <span class="av-tag ok">available</span>
            <h3>GitHub</h3>
            <ul class="av-list">
              <li>github.com</li>
              <li>Enterprise Cloud</li>
              <li>Enterprise Managed Users</li>
              <li>Enterprise Server <span class="av-note">self-hosted</span></li>
            </ul>
          </div>
          <div class="av-card">
            <span class="av-tag ok">available</span>
            <h3>Azure DevOps</h3>
            <ul class="av-list">
              <li>Services</li>
              <li>Server <span class="av-note">self-hosted</span></li>
            </ul>
          </div>
          <div class="av-card">
            <span class="av-tag ok">available</span>
            <h3>Browsers</h3>
            <ul class="av-list">
              <li>Chrome, Edge, Brave <span class="av-note">and other Chromium-based browsers</span></li>
              <li>Firefox <span class="av-note">separate build</span></li>
            </ul>
          </div>
        </div>
      </div>
    </section>

    <section class="hband sunk">
      <div class="in">
        <div class="stack g14 measure">
          <span class="eyebrow">not yet:</span>
          <h2>Other platforms</h2>
          <p style="font-size:15px;color:var(--fg-2);line-height:1.6">The convention itself works anywhere — it's simply a way of writing comments. What isn't available everywhere yet is the tooling: the toolbar, validation, and the pull request check.</p>
        </div>
        <div class="avail">
          <div class="av-card soon">
            <span class="av-tag">to come</span>
            <h3>GitLab</h3>
            <p class="av-p">Merge requests on gitlab.com and self-managed instances.</p>
          </div>
          <div class="av-card soon">
            <span class="av-tag">to come</span>
            <h3>Bitbucket</h3>
            <p class="av-p">Cloud and Data Center.</p>
          </div>
          <div class="av-card soon">
            <span class="av-tag">tell us</span>
            <h3>Need another platform?</h3>
            <p class="av-p">Open an issue on the repository and tell us which platform you use and roughly how many people would benefit from it. We use that information to decide what to support next.</p>
          </div>
        </div>
        <p class="small measure">We don't promise dates. A platform is only listed as available once it actually works.</p>
      </div>
    </section>

    <section class="hband">
      <div class="in">
        <div class="split">
          <div class="stack g14">
            <span class="eyebrow">what you need:</span>
            <h2 style="font-size:24px">The extension needs no infrastructure.</h2>
            <p style="font-size:15px;color:var(--fg-2);line-height:1.6">Install it and it works immediately: the toolbar, shortcuts, and live feedback. No account, no server, no configuration required. The store listings haven't been published yet, so for now the extension is installed from a release archive. The installation page provides browser-specific instructions.</p>
            <p style="font-size:15px;color:var(--fg-2);line-height:1.6">The pull request check is the only part that requires a server: a small service you host once for the entire organization. You only need it if your team wants to make the convention a merge requirement.</p>
            <div class="hero-btns">
              <a class="btn btn-p" href={href('install', 'en')}>Install the extension</a>
              <a class="btn btn-s" href={href('adoption', 'en')}>When the server is worth it</a>
            </div>
          </div>
          <div class="stack g14">
            <span class="eyebrow">and where it runs:</span>
            <h2 style="font-size:24px">github.com only, by default.</h2>
            <p style="font-size:15px;color:var(--fg-2);line-height:1.6">The extension doesn't request access to every site. By default, it has access to one domain: github.com. Azure DevOps, a self-hosted GitHub instance, or an internal host each requires separate permission. Your browser asks for that permission, and you choose whether to grant it.</p>
            <p class="small">Two permissions in total: one to remember your preferences and one to work inside the page. No account. No stored token. The extension cannot load code from anywhere else. That restriction is enforced technically and verified again with every release.</p>
          </div>
        </div>
      </div>
    </section>

    <section class="cta-band">
      <div class="cta-in">
        <div class="stack g8">
          <h2>If your platform is listed, you can start today.</h2>
          <p class="small" style="color:var(--fg-2)">Nothing becomes blocking until your team decides it should.</p>
        </div>
        <div class="hero-btns">
          <a class="btn btn-p" href={href('install', 'en')}>Install the extension</a>
          <a class="btn btn-s" href={href('labels', 'en')}>See the labels</a>
        </div>
      </div>
    </section>
</Base>
